\",\n      \"Used In\",\n      \"Contemporary Western Societies\",\n      \"In contemporary Western societies, tattoos continue to serve as symbols of personal identity and self-expression, often used to commemorate important moments and changes in life.\"\n    ],\n    [\n      \"Tattoos\",\n      \"Associated With\",\n      \"Personal Identity\",\n      \"Tattoos are often used as a way for individuals to express their unique identities, values, interests, and experiences.\"\n    ],\n    [\n      \"Tattoos\",\n      \"Associated With\",\n      \"Cultural Heritage\",\n      \"Tattoos are seen as a way to connect individuals to their cultural heritage and community traditions in many indigenous cultures.\"\n    ],\n    [\n      \"Tattoos\",\n      \"Associated With\",\n      \"Ritual and Ceremony\",\n      \"Tattoos have been used in rituals and ceremonies in many cultures to mark significant life events and milestones.\"\n    ],\n    [\n      \"natural world\",\n      \"Influences\",\n      \"tattoos\",\n      \"The natural world influences tattoo designs, which often incorporate elements and symbols from nature to reinforce the individual\\\"s connection to their environment.\"\n    ],\n    [\n      \"natural world\",\n      \"Reinforces Bond With\",\n      \"individuals\",\n      \"The natural world reinforces the bond between individuals and their environment through the use of nature-inspired tattoo designs.\"\n    ],\n    [\n      \"natural world\",\n      \"Reinforces Bond With\",\n      \"community\",\n      \"The natural world reinforces the bond between a community and its environment, often reflected in the shared tattoo designs that symbolize this connection.\"\n    ],\n    [\n      \"Japanese culture\",\n      \"Includes\",\n      \"Yakuza\",\n      \"Japanese culture includes the yakuza, who traditionally used tattoos as symbols of loyalty, courage, and strength.\"\n    ],\n    [\n      \"Japanese culture\",\n      \"Influences\",\n      \"Ainu People\"